cker who owns the   machine could bypass this"; "an operator could edit the file to defeat it"; "this hook is   bypassable with `git push --no-verify`" (known — the real gate is server-side); "this doesn't   defend against a malicious insider." Those are the wrong threat model.  ## THREE-LAYER ENFORCEMENT ARCHITECTURE (so you don't mis-flag advisory-by-design pieces) 1. **`[C]` client skill (portable plugin):** agent judgment + operator UX. **Advisory / best-effort    by nature** — it is guidance the agent follows, not a hard gate. It is NOT the security boundary. 2. **`[S]` server:** stock GitHub **branch-protection + CODEOWNERS + required status checks +    secret push-protection**, plus a tiny custom CI aggregator. This is the real teeth. It    **RE-DERIVES its verdicts from the diff server-side and never trusts a client artifact.**    It is **GH-Teams-gated: ADVISORY until the org is on GitHub Team, enforced after.** So "this    control doesn't block yet" is expected and correct for now. 3. **Env-floor:** the agent runtime holds only a scoped non-admin `gh` token + an S3 public-image    bucket. No prod DB / live payment·comms keys / SSH / deploy. The out-of-git-effects footgun    class cannot happen because the credential isn't present.  The RED-GLOB MANIFEST is the **path-matchable** red set only (paths where ANY change is "red" = held for human review). Semantic red that is NOT path-matchable (IDOR, a hidden surcharge, a CSS change that unlocks a guarded control) is deliberately **out of the manifest** — it is caught by a separate effect-classifier at merge. So "the manifest doesn't catch <semantic issue>" is BY DESIGN, not a gap — unless the issue is genuinely path-matchable and a path is missing.  ## WHAT IS BUILT SO FAR (the artifacts under review) A "prereq layer" for the pipeline, plus one change to Mark's machine-local git-identity hook: - **S0 `HARNESS.md`** — a build-time contract/vocabulary doc every later component is authored   against (state names,